id: pm-w7mqzt
title: "Extension authors cannot consume the SDK's own flag, filter and loader contracts, so every first-party package hand-rewrites the baseline the CLI already declares"
description: "The stated architecture is that the CLI delivers baseline primitives through the SDK so any package can reuse them. For the command-authoring surface that is not yet true: the primitives exist, they are exported, and they are shaped so that no extension can use them.\n\nFinding 1, the filter-flag baseline is unconsumable. src/sdk/cli-contracts/flag-contracts.ts exports LIST_FILTER_FLAG_CONTRACTS, the canonical 20-plus entry declaration of --status, --type, --tag, --priority, the deadline and updated and created windows, --ids, --assignee, --parent and the rest, complete with alias and cursor semantics, and it is public through the sdk/contracts subpath. api.registerCommand takes FlagDefinition, declared in src/core/extensions/extension-types.ts:759 with the fields long, short, value_name, description, required, enabled. CliFlagContract is a different shape entirely — flag, list, aliases. A repository-wide search finds no adapter in either direction. The canonical declaration is therefore visible to package authors and unusable by them.\n\nThe consequence is measurable. pm-calendar, pm-search-advanced and pm-governance-audit each hand-write their own --type, --tag, --priority, --status FlagDefinition blocks; jscpd at 12 lines / 60 tokens reports them as clones of one another (pm-calendar/index.ts:71 against pm-search-advanced/index.ts:164 and against pm-governance-audit/index.ts:129). They have already drifted: pm-search-advanced carries hand-added snake_case aliases such as --phrase_exact that no other package has, because there is nothing to inherit alias policy from. Every future filter flag the CLI adds has to be re-typed into each package or the package silently falls behind the baseline.\n\nFinding 2, the runtime loader is copied rather than provided. Ten of the fifteen first-party packages contain their own ensureRuntimeBundle / loadRuntimeBundle that reads PM_CLI_PACKAGE_ROOT, joins dist/sdk paths by hand, pathToFileURL-imports the result, and validates an allow-list of expected export names. pm-beads and pm-todos are clones of each other three times over; pm-guide-shell and pm-linked-test-adapters likewise. PM_CLI_PACKAGE_ROOT is documented as first-party-only, which is a correct boundary, but the effect is that the loader problem it solves is unsolved for everyone else and re-solved by hand ten times inside the repository. A related consequence worth stating plainly: only two of fifteen first-party packages import SDK values through the public export map, so the published subpaths are barely dogfooded by the packages best placed to dogfood them.\n\nFinding 3, the exemplar is not a kit. packages/pm-command-kit is described in its own manifest as an exemplar for the commands capability. It demonstrates registerCommand, registerFlags and registerParser; it exports nothing an author can build on. pm-kanban references it as a model to copy, not as a dependency.\n\nTaken together these are the difference between an SDK that documents how to write a package and an SDK that supplies what a package needs. Closing it removes duplication that already exists, prevents the divergence that has already started, and is a precondition for third-party packages matching first-party capability without reverse-engineering the repository.\n\nScope boundary. pm-9smp7j and pm-c5f0gh own scaffold generation quality; this is about what the scaffolded package can then import. pm-x6jf owns package and extension lifecycle promotion. pm-usfg owns the SDK-first inversion in general; this is its command-authoring slice, and it is the slice a package author meets first."
type: Feature
status: open
priority: 2
tags[5]: "area:extensions","area:sdk",customization,dx,refactor
created_at: "2026-07-25T21:40:27.254Z"
updated_at: "2026-07-26T14:09:14.479Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 420
acceptance_criteria: "An extension can declare the standard filter-flag set by consuming an SDK export rather than by restating FlagDefinition objects, and adding a filter flag to the CLI baseline reaches consuming packages without per-package edits. The SDK provides the runtime-resolution helper that the ten first-party copies of ensureRuntimeBundle currently implement, so no package hand-joins dist paths; the first-party copies are replaced by it. Alias and cursor semantics attached to a baseline flag are inherited rather than re-declared, and pm-search-advanced's divergent aliases are either adopted into the baseline or dropped. At least one first-party package is migrated end to end as proof and its hand-written tables are deleted. A regression test asserts that a package built only against public SDK subpaths can register a command carrying the full baseline filter set."
parent: pm-ugqx
risk: medium
confidence: high
