notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-26T11:33:06.917Z","harness:claude-code","Positive control on the scaffold path, run 2026-07-26 on the shipped 2026.7.26 build, recorded to bound this item's scope precisely.\n\nThree commands take a package from nothing to a working command, with no npm install and no build step:\n\n  pm package --init demo-tracker      -> 7 files (package.json, manifest.json, index.ts, index.test.ts, tsconfig.json, .gitignore, README.md)\n  pm install --project ./demo-tracker -> activation_status ok, activated true, registered_commands ['demo tracker ping'], registered_actions ['demo-tracker-ping'], health ok, 0 blocking failures\n  pm demo tracker ping                -> ok: true, message 'Starter extension scaffold is active.'\n\nThe native type-stripping loader means a .ts entry runs directly, so the scaffold's promise is real and the getting-started experience is genuinely three commands. The next_steps block returned by --init lists install, smoke test, typecheck, test, and doctor in the right order.\n\nThe consequence for this item: the gap is not onboarding, it is the second step. A package author who accepts the generated shape is well served; one who needs a real flag surface hits the missing adapter between LIST_FILTER_FLAG_CONTRACTS/CliFlagContract and registerCommand's FlagDefinition that this item owns. Framing the work as 'the scaffold stops one layer short of the contracts the CLI itself uses' is more accurate than 'authoring is hard', and it keeps the fix scoped to the adapter rather than to the generator.\n\nIncidental observation, not a defect claim: a hyphenated target produces a two-token command path (demo-tracker -> 'pm demo tracker'). That is consistent with the noun-verb grammar; the pm-prefixed variant of the same derivation is pm-c5f0gh's subject."
